---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Independent Assurance for Serious Software — MathGraph"
  description="Your tests passed. But did they test the right thing? A source-bound, human-led independent assurance audit for consequential software."
  currentPath="/assurance/"
>
  <header class="section section--compact">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">MathGraph · Independent assurance for serious software</div>
      <h1>Your tests passed. But did they test the right thing?</h1>
      <p class="lede">AI helps you climb faster. MathGraph checks whether your ladder is against the right wall—and shows you the evidence.</p>
      <p>Beyond finding bugs, we challenge the assumptions behind your code: what it is supposed to do, what has actually been tested, and what the evidence does or does not establish.</p>
      <p><strong>Know what you don't know.</strong></p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="#audit-scope">What an audit delivers</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href="#evidence">Inspect a real case</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title">
    <div class="shell grid">
      <div class="span-5">
        <div class="eyebrow">Independent technical decision · public evidence</div>
        <h2 id="evidence-title">A test-reporting path that could show green when it should fail.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="span-7">
        <p>In NASA JPL's open-source L4YAML project, an inconsistent JUnit XML acceptance boundary allowed malformed report content to be accepted by the Lean report reader where a separate Python consumer rejected it. That mismatch could let a success report obscure a reporting failure.</p>
        <p>We developed a reproducible source-level correction. The upstream maintainer independently reproduced the defect, reviewed the repair, approved it, and merged <a href="https://github.com/nasa-jpl/L4YAML/pull/232" rel="external">PR #232</a>.</p>
        <p>Independent native qualifications include <a href="https://github.com/metalogiclabs/L4YAML/actions/runs/41979488458" rel="external">24/24 positive and negative XML controls and the full integrated suite on an earlier candidate source</a>. Because the final merged revision differs, its own <a href="https://github.com/metalogiclabs/L4YAML/actions/runs/42274503860" rel="external">exact-source replay</a> must be assessed separately.</p>
        <div class="callout callout--unknown">
          <p><strong>Evidence boundary:</strong> This is a real, externally reviewed open-source contribution. It is not a claim of NASA/JPL organizational endorsement, a paid engagement, an autonomous discovery by the deployed MathGraph Check App, or a safety certification. Untested behavior remains <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--compact" id="audit-scope" aria-labelledby="audit-scope-title" data-assurance-boundary>
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Human-led independent audit · fixed scope</div>
      <h2 id="audit-scope-title">A small, consequential question. An inspectable answer.</h2>
      <p class="lede">For aerospace, robotics, autonomous systems, scientific computing, and other serious software where an unexamined assumption matters.</p>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="span-6 callout">
          <h3>What we investigate</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>One important behavior, invariant, requirement, or proposed code change.</li>
            <li>The gap between stated intent, implemented behavior, and existing tests.</li>
            <li>Adversarial boundaries, alternative interpretations, and falsifying cases.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="span-6 callout">
          <h3>What you receive</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Precisely identified source revisions and review assumptions.</li>
            <li>Reproducible counterexamples or independently rerunnable controls where available.</li>
            <li>Evidence-supported conclusions, unresolved questions, and a bounded recommendation.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">An audit can find a defect, validate a claim within a declared boundary, or return UNKNOWN. It is not a certification, a formal proof of the entire system, or automatic release approval. The scope and evidence sources are agreed before work starts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--compact" aria-labelledby="enquiry-title">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Request an independent audit</div>
      <h2 id="enquiry-title">Start with the decision you need to make.</h2>
      <p>Ask for a one-change or one-requirement scope discussion. No source upload or customer login is offered on this page. Do not send proprietary code or confidential system information through public GitHub issues.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="https://nz.linkedin.com/in/hesanchez" rel="external">Request a private scope discussion ↗</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href="/check/">Inspect Check's published evidence boundaries</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">Initial enquiries only; source handling, confidentiality, scope, cost, and evidence access must be agreed separately. This is a human-led service at the present research stage.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
